--- a/packages/client/ui-trajectory/README.md
+++ b/packages/client/ui-trajectory/README.md
@@ -37,6 +37,14 @@ Summary and Preview share one ordered attachment list after the message text, pr
 
 Thinking in the inspector uses compact Markdown at the inspector's fixed 13px size and 20px line height, independent of the content-size setting. Headings add bold weight without increasing size or line height. Assistant output keeps its regular Markdown typography.
 
+### Record annotations
+
+The original ledger declares `conversation.trajectory.column` and `conversation.trajectory.inspector.objects` as additive Session list slots. A column contribution registers `id`, `order`, and a locale-aware `label`; Trajectory owns each table header, cell, width, and shared virtual-row span, and dispatches one winning contribution per column with the Slot renderer's `only` option. Object contributions appear below the existing Overview without replacing input, output, schema, or timing tabs. Removing all contributions restores the original two-column table and inspector while preserving the selected record.
+
+Both locations receive the type-only [TrajectoryRecordContext](src/client/trajectory-extension-contract.ts): a branded logical record identity, the original seven kinds, durable event addresses and known call ancestry. Event addresses contain the owning branded SessionId and an integer logical sequence; live-only records have empty addresses and `transient: true`. Sorting anchors and row indexes never become durable addresses. The owner data contains no body, result metadata, components, or functions; annotation consumers resolve original events through their existing authorized readers.
+
+The `useTrajectory` source also publishes `recordContexts` for logical records in the loaded Session event window, before the table's 50-node paging, search, or folding. This JSON list is cached with the target snapshot identity and uses the same address derivation as the original table. It does not open another event stream or assert that unloaded history has been inspected.
+
 ### The timing overview
 
 Historical replies retain TTFT, generation duration, and throughput when their recorded streams contain token timestamps. TTFT measures from the Step start to its first token, including output from an earlier retry attempt; an unloaded Step start or a stream without tokens leaves the corresponding metric unavailable.
